{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q019_stable_periodic_release_semantics","answer":"The current-release endpoint (/api/v1/releases/current) reports SCOPe 2.08 as the current public release. Its stable label is "2.08-stable" (classification frozen 2021-07-28, released 2021-09-22; is_public true, is_comprehensive false, no old_version). The latest periodic update reported is version "2.08-2026-08-16", dated 2026-08-16.

For a reproducible analysis you should cite the frozen stable identifier, "2.08-stable" (i.e. SCOPe release 2.08 as frozen on 2021-07-28), and pin every API call to release=2.08 rather than relying on the unpinned "current" alias. The stable release is the fixed snapshot of curated classification (SUNIDs, SCCS codes, SIDs, domain boundaries) that does not change after the freeze date.

The periodic label (2.08-YYYY-MM-DD) denotes a dated incremental update layered on top of the 2.08 stable classification. Periodic updates add newly released PDB entries/chains that can be classified automatically against the frozen 2.08 hierarchy (and supporting derived data such as ASTRAL/quality fields and similarity hits), without altering the stable classification tree itself. Because the periodic version string changes with each update, citing it alone (or citing "current") is not stable over time; if your analysis depends on entries added after the freeze, cite both the stable release (2.08-stable) and the exact periodic update date (2026-08-16) so the data set can be reconstructed. Note also that is_comprehensive is false, meaning 2.08 does not cover every PDB entry, so absence of an entry from SCOPe 2.08 is not evidence that it lacks a classifiable domain.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","/api/v1/","/api/v1/releases/current"]}]}
